---
description: Use .lib.ts files for executable scripts so the core logic is testable and reusable.
globs: 'scripts/**/*.ts'
alwaysApply: true
---

Split executable scripts into two files:

- **`scriptName.lib.ts`** — the core function (exported, testable, importable).
- **`scriptName.lib.test.ts`** — tests for the core function.
- **`scriptName.ts`** — thin executable runner that parses CLI args and calls the lib function.

This keeps the script importable without side effects (no top-level process exit or console logging).

## Example

```typescript
// scripts/cron/cleanup.lib.ts
export async function cleanup(options: { dryRun: boolean }): Promise<void> {
  // core logic
}

// scripts/cron/cleanup.ts
import { cleanup } from './cleanup.lib';

async function main() {
  const { dryRun } = parseArgs();
  await cleanup({ dryRun });
}

main().catch((err) => { console.error(err); process.exit(1); });
```

## Why

- `scriptName.ts` files are meant to be run directly (via `yarn swc-node`). Importing them executes side effects immediately.
- `.lib.ts` files can be safely imported from other modules, including tests.
- Core logic in `.lib.ts` gets a `.lib.test.ts` companion for unit tests.
